session:
  updated: 2026-08-04
  status: review_findings_addressed
  branch: main

goal:
  current: address the findings from the overall template review and close the gaps they exposed

completed:
  - fixed project ready specialisation detection: it parsed `project --list`, which
    always prints the union of COMMANDS and UNSPECIALISED, so the check could never
    be false and a specialised project would run unspecialised targets and fail
  - extracted select_runtime_commands so the selection is directly testable
  - wired the missing `project build` step into readiness; ordered lint, build, test
  - aligned ci and static-analysis skills with the AGENTS.md rule that lint precedes test
  - fixed 7 broken sibling cross-references between specialise skills (bare ci/SKILL.md
    resolved relative to the referring skill's own directory, not to specialise/)
  - backfilled Build, Static analysis, Testing and Ecosystem openness sections for
    runtime-python, runtime-node, runtime-elixir, runtime-godot; also jvm, perl, php
  - added a discovery-driven test holding every runtime skill to the same pattern, so a
    runtime added later cannot ship thin
  - added test_project_ready.py covering the detection bug that shipped undetected
  - added Anthropic and OpenAI project-scoped key patterns to check-secrets; the plain
    sk- pattern could not match either because of the hyphen after the prefix
  - scoped check-secrets to git-committable files, so gitignored paths are out of scope
    and the tool's own advice to move secrets into an untracked .env is coherent
  - ran a full-history scan over 759 blobs across all commits; no credentials found
  - fixed the scaffold reporting directories as changed on every run without comparing
    contents
  - specified and tested the wiki axis rule: no axis directory means pre-adoption and is
    not checked; any axis directory means adoption, and every page must migrate
  - replaced the fixed pre-commit hook with a declarative gate: .agents/hooks.toon
    declares checks, run-hooks executes them concurrently with per-check timeouts,
    blocking or advisory per check, skipping unspecialised project targets
  - promoted the five verified-resolved duplication candidates to enforced topics
  - added why-this-not-a-skill.md and elevated the knowledge-graph framing in README
  - refined why-this-not-a-skill.md for engineers, agentic developers and newcomers:
    seven-point case, ontology extensibility and provenance, bounded subagents,
    cross-agent contract, Superpowers composition boundary, repeatable-project
    pattern, opinionated scaffolds, honest costs and a domain-focus close
  - added ontology, subagent, topology and tooling-detection terms to the glossary
  - added outside-in-tdd/verification.md: prove every test can fail before treating it
    as evidence; red-first for new code, mutation check for tests written after it,
    reproduce-first for regressions, break-the-input for tooling checks; declared as a
    skill layer so the router preloads it on guarded work
  - triaged all worktrees, local branches and upstream PRs; found
    worktrees/codex-git-worktree-model stale (branch tip == old main from 2026-07-11,
    zero unique commits, 20 commits behind) with uncommitted WIP superseded by the
    already-shipped worktree-status skill
  - ported the reusable part of that WIP (the start/work/integrate/cleanup procedure;
    its check-worktrees validator and active_work: schema were not portable, and its
    unqualified worktrees/ path was superseded by .worktrees/) into a new
    coordination/git-worktree-workflow skill, catalogued alongside worktree-status
  - backed up the discarded diff and untracked files to scratchpad, then removed the
    worktree and deleted the stale local branch (never pushed, so nothing lost upstream)
  - merged proactive-advisory-skills into main: adds context-hygiene-advisory,
    specialist-recruitment-advisory and loop-engineering-advisory coordination skills,
    plus the optional loop_state HANDOFF field they use; merged directly by explicit
    request rather than via PR, since the branch was clean, self-reviewed and had no
    conflicts with main
  - resolved the merge conflicts by hand: check-repo-contract and handoff.schema.md
    were pure additive collisions (combined both); README.md's branch-side skill-table
    edit was fully superseded by main's later switch to a short curated table plus
    CATALOG.toon as the single source of truth, so kept HEAD as-is; HANDOFF.toon kept
    HEAD's current state, folding the branch's still-true decisions and risks in
  - re-ran the full suite post-merge: 325 tests, project check, project ready all pass
  - deleted local branches now fully landed on main: proactive-advisory-skills (merged
    above), feat/portable-context-router and feature/agentic-lifecycle (both verified
    byte-identical to their PR #5/#4 merge commits, which are ancestors of main) -
    removed their worktree/checkouts too where present, so nothing stale is left lying
    around
  - mutation-checked the highest-risk slice of the pre-existing suite (325 tests
    predate the "prove it can fail" rule; this is a bounded first pass, not the whole
    suite): triaged all 12 test files by risk, then genuinely broke-and-restored the
    implementation behind check-secrets (13/13 tests: pattern detection, git-aware
    scope, wiring into project/check-repo-contract), project-ready's runtime-command
    selection (7/11: the exact historical bug class - filter removed, is_template
    inverted, build dropped, label duplicated), and the pre-commit gate itself
    (15/22 of test_hooks.py: blocking vs advisory, concurrency, ordering, timeout,
    missing-command handling, no-config handling, token rendering, the foreign-hook
    install guard, secrets-must-block in the real .agents/hooks.toon); every mutation
    was confirmed red, restored, and confirmed green again; working tree and full
    325-test suite both clean at the end
  - found and fixed a gap: test_template_state_runs_no_runtime_commands
    (test_project_ready.py) did not independently catch inverting the `is_template`
    short-circuit - it stayed green by coincidence because today's dispatcher
    UNSPECIALISED set already excludes every runtime command anyway. Fixed by passing
    set() instead of dispatcher.UNSPECIALISED, isolating the branch under test;
    re-ran the is_template-inversion mutation and confirmed it now goes red
  - continued the mutation-check sweep onto test_scaffold_acceptance.py and
    test_toon.py, fixing gaps found rather than just cataloguing them:
    - wrote a missing regression test for the scaffold change-detection bug fixed in
      02d2463 - that commit's message and HANDOFF both claimed a fix, but only the
      wiki-axis half of it got a test; test_reapplying_scaffold_to_an_unchanged_
      project_reports_no_updates now guards it, reproduced against the pre-fix
      behaviour (falsely reports "2 update" for the two directory entries) before
      confirming it passes on current code
    - mutation-checked the wiki axis rule end to end (pre-adoption bypass, adoption
      trigger, per-page frontmatter/axis-directory mismatch) and the byte-identical
      copy guarantee; all caught their mutations
    - mutation-checked test_toon.py's scalar coercion, quote detection, nested-map
      indent, key-vs-scalar-line disambiguation (including the URL double-slash
      guard), duplicate-key last-wins, and the two structural error checks
    - found and fixed test_odd_indent_is_rejected_with_line_number: it only asserted
      "line 2" appears in the error, which a completely different, unrelated
      "unexpected indent" fallback also satisfies (every valid indent is even, so an
      odd one always trips something); strengthened to assert the specific "not a
      multiple of two" message, matching its sibling tab-indent test's pattern
    - found and fixed a real hole in the escaping round trip: the only backslash
      value in test_awkward_scalars_survive_the_round_trip ("back\slash") never
      triggers _needs_quoting (no colon, no leading bracket/quote char), so it
      round-trips bare and _quote()'s backslash/quote-escaping was never exercised
      despite the module's own docstring promising it; added
      test_backslash_and_quote_survive_a_value_that_also_needs_quoting with a value
      that needs quoting for an unrelated reason (a colon) and also contains both
      characters _quote() must escape
    - hit and resolved a test-infra hazard mid-sweep: rapid edit/test/restore cycles
      on the same file within the same filesystem-mtime tick can serve a stale
      __pycache__ .pyc instead of recompiling the restored source, producing a
      false "still red after restore" result; cleared __pycache__ and switched to
      `python3 -B` (no bytecode cache) for the rest of the sweep. Source content was
      verified correct via `git diff` throughout, and the two full-suite runs
      bracketing the earlier check-secrets/project-ready/hooks work both came back
      clean at the expected count, so that earlier work is not in question - this
      was caught by, not hidden from, the restore-verification step
  - full suite: 327 tests (was 325; +1 scaffold regression test, +1 toon escaping
    test), all pass, working tree left clean after every mutation cycle

in_progress: []

next:
  - reconcile local branch research-driven-specialise (approved spec, last touched
    2026-07-16, no worktree, not reflected in this HANDOFF) - resume or park explicitly
  - apply the shift-left pipeline and context router to a real project via /specialise
  - read why-this-not-a-skill.md end to end and adjust tone where it overreaches
  - continue the mutation-check sweep on the remaining ~275 pre-existing tests, in
    this risk order: test_router_precedence.py and test_taxonomy_check.py (core
    context-router mechanisms), then test_qualification.py, test_explain.py,
    test_observations.py, test_environment.py, test_skill_layers.py; lowest priority
    is the ~93 doc-presence/wiring tests in test_static_analysis.py (skill file
    exists / mentions X / is catalogued) plus the remaining ~24 tests already
    partially covered this session (test_scaffold_acceptance.py's AC2-AC14 profile/
    recovery/qualification tests and catalog-merge/domain-skill tests,
    test_project_ready.py's 4 TestDispatcherIsTheSourceOfTruth tests,
    test_hooks.py's TestHooksConfig structure checks and 3 of TestInstaller)
  - promote remaining inbox proposals (INBOX-001..009) as evidence accumulates
  - watch loop_state blocks for staleness: they must be removed once a fallback loop
    completes or they read as abandoned work
  - consider adding PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 (or an equivalent) to the test-running
    path given the __pycache__ staleness hazard found this session; not done yet
    since it was worked around locally and hasn't caused a real false pass, only a
    false red that was itself caught

decisions:
  - id: portable_context_router
    summary: route context depth and timing by profile, never required behaviour
  - id: capability_by_observation_not_registry
    summary: license lean context from fixture-scored behaviour, never from model identity
  - id: wiki_method_product_axis
    summary: every wiki page declares axis method or product, matching its directory
  - id: wiki_axis_adoption_is_all_or_nothing
    summary: a wholly flat wiki is unchecked; creating one axis directory requires migrating every page
  - id: yaml_skill_frontmatter_deviation
    summary: keep skill frontmatter in YAML despite the TOON default for state
  - id: static_analysis_from_specialise
    summary: every generated project makes an explicit static-analysis decision at /specialise
  - id: nix_first_build_pipeline
    summary: every generated project makes an explicit build decision at /specialise; Nix owns the toolchain
  - id: deployment_pipeline_from_specialise
    summary: every generated project records a deployment pipeline decision at /specialise
  - id: observability_from_specialise
    summary: every generated project records an observability decision at /specialise
  - id: declarative_precommit_gate
    summary: hook checks are declared in project-owned .agents/hooks.toon, run concurrently, blocking per check
  - id: secret_scan_scoped_to_committable_files
    summary: scan what git would let you commit; gitignored paths are out of scope, history needs a real scanner
  - id: tests_must_be_proven_able_to_fail
    summary: a test observed only passing is not evidence; prove it fails, then restore and rerun
  - id: worktree_procedure_is_a_skill_not_just_prose
    summary: >-
      the concrete start/work/integrate/cleanup steps for an agent worktree live in
      coordination/git-worktree-workflow, alongside the read-only worktree-status
      report; AGENTS.md keeps only the rules (never remove dirty, .worktrees/ location,
      one per agent), not the commands
  - id: three_separate_skills
    summary: >-
      loop-engineering-advisory, specialist-recruitment-advisory and
      context-hygiene-advisory are three focused skills, not one unified skill,
      matching existing coordination skill granularity
  - id: loop_state_disk_persisted
    summary: >-
      loop-engineering fallback tracks iteration state in an optional HANDOFF.toon
      loop_state block so it survives context resets, session restarts or
      model/provider swaps mid-loop
  - id: no_new_bin_scripts
    summary: >-
      all three advisory skills are judgment/instruction skills like team-selection
      and agent-team-fallback; no new .agentic-template/bin/ command
  - id: presence_only_testing
    summary: >-
      advisory skills are validated by check-repo-contract presence and frontmatter
      checks only, no new self-test fixtures, matching how other coordination skills
      are tested
  - id: specialist_recruitment_vs_team_selection
    summary: >-
      specialist-recruitment-advisory is the earlier mid-task tripwire that surfaces
      an explicit offer; team-selection still owns the actual roster mechanics once
      accepted

risks:
  - runtime skill tool tables may go stale; mitigated by revisit_trigger and openness sections
  - the pre-commit gate is only as fast as its checks; a project adding a slow blocking
    check will push people toward --no-verify
  - check-secrets still does not scan git history; a full scanner is needed before any
    repository with real history is published
  - no real generated project has exercised the shift-left pipeline yet
  - positioning copy in why-this-not-a-skill.md is a first draft, not the owner's voice
  - advisory skills could become noisy if triggers are too broad; triggers are scoped
    narrowly (repeating pattern detected / specialist domain matched with none engaged /
    context growing or boundary reached)
  - ~290 of 325 pre-existing tests still have not been mutation-checked (only
    check-secrets, most of project-ready, and most of run-hooks/install-hooks have been
    verified so far); an untested test in that remainder could be silently vacuous
    until its file comes up in the ordered sweep in next
  - test_template_state_runs_no_runtime_commands (test_project_ready.py) does not
    independently guard the is_template short-circuit it names; only a coincidence of
    today's UNSPECIALISED set keeps it correct, and two sibling tests carry the real
    coverage

